WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.433 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:02.569 align:middle line:84%
Do you remember learning
about modern natural right

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:05.138 align:middle line:84%
from Professor Baer
before spring break?

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.406 align:middle line:90%
Yes, a bit.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.975 align:middle line:84%
We studied Locke's state of
nature and its connection

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.112 align:middle line:84%
with natural right, if that's
what you're talking about.

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:13.747 align:middle line:84%
Professor Baer
also made the case

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:17.283 align:middle line:84%
that modern natural right is the
basis for the American regime

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.486 align:middle line:84%
and the reason for
this country's success.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:19.986 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.888 align:middle line:84%
And yes, that is
exactly what I mean.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:23.390 align:middle line:84%
It's great that you
remember reading

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.025 align:middle line:84%
Locke and about
modern natural right

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.728 align:middle line:84%
being the basis for
the American regime.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:29.229 align:middle line:90%
Oh.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.630 align:middle line:90%
Hello, Professor Knowles.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.865 align:middle line:90%
What are you doing here?

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.967 align:middle line:90%
I hope I'm not bothering you.

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.536 align:middle line:84%
I heard that you were
discussing modern natural right,

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.571 align:middle line:90%
and I wanted to join.

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:39.906 align:middle line:90%
Please do join us.

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:43.109 align:middle line:84%
We were just starting
on modern natural right.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:48.681 align:middle line:84%
So how does Locke view the
problem of AI and bias?

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:49.582 align:middle line:90%
Patch.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.552 align:middle line:84%
Locke would think it
wrong because bias

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.621 align:middle line:90%
violates your natural right.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.423 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I agree.

00:00:56.423 --> 00:01:00.293 align:middle line:84%
That sounds right,
but I'm not sure

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:04.631 align:middle line:84%
because we said that justice is
more important than equality.

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:09.202 align:middle line:84%
But it seems that equality
is fundamental in Locke.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:12.305 align:middle line:84%
But then where is
justice in Locke?

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.874 align:middle line:90%
Let's take this step by step.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.509 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone remember
the beginning?

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:19.212 align:middle line:90%


00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.214 align:middle line:84%
But you said you were
working on these questions

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.183 align:middle line:90%
during spring break.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:23.783 align:middle line:90%
Alvis?

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:27.887 align:middle line:84%
Well, we were discussing them,
but we never actually went back

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.023 align:middle line:90%
to the texts from class.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:30.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.294 align:middle line:84%
We just wanted to figure
them out for ourselves.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.564 align:middle line:84%
I guess you disapprove,
Professor Muller?

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.032 align:middle line:90%
On the contrary.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:40.800 align:middle line:84%
You are a very
impressive group to have

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.869 align:middle line:84%
stayed here over spring
break and investigated

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:43.803 align:middle line:90%
these questions.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:47.107 align:middle line:84%
Any teacher would be thrilled to
have even one student like you,

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:48.808 align:middle line:90%
and I have five.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.009 align:middle line:90%
But you said that you got stuck.

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:53.012 align:middle line:84%
What was your plan to
get out of your impasse?

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.848 align:middle line:90%
Well, your class, of course.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.082 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:57.150 align:middle line:90%
Hush, Patch.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.019 align:middle line:84%
You seem not to realize
that Professor Muller is

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.522 align:middle line:84%
in a most mild way
rebuking us for not

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:04.691 align:middle line:90%
using the resources at hand.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.893 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's use them now.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.662 align:middle line:84%
Professor, please remind us
of Locke's starting point.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:10.630 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.166 align:middle line:84%
Let's turn to second
treatise, chapter 1.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.035 align:middle line:90%


00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:18.872 align:middle line:84%
Professor Knowles, where do
you think we should start?

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:21.674 align:middle line:84%
I'd say the last section
of paragraph one,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.843 align:middle line:90%
starting with "he that."

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.678 align:middle line:84%
That is exactly
what I was thinking.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.480 align:middle line:84%
Glenda, could you
read the statement

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.549 align:middle line:90%
starting with "he that?"

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:30.783 align:middle line:90%
Of course.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.819 align:middle line:84%
He that will not
give just occasion

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.554 align:middle line:84%
to think that all
government in the world

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.623 align:middle line:84%
is the product of only
force and violence

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.526 align:middle line:84%
must of necessity find out
another rise of government.

00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:40.894 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:43.329 align:middle line:84%
So Locke is giving
us two options

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:47.267 align:middle line:84%
of the origin of government,
force or something else--

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.302 align:middle line:84%
maybe something
else based on reason

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:52.105 align:middle line:84%
but not narrow or
mathematical reason.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.305 align:middle line:90%
Yes, of course.

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:54.908 align:middle line:90%
That is much better.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.377 align:middle line:84%
It would be terrible to
think that politics is just

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.311 align:middle line:90%
about force.

00:02:58.311 --> 00:02:59.245 align:middle line:90%
I agree.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.680 align:middle line:84%
It should be about
allowing people

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.383 align:middle line:90%
to be happy, as we said before.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:07.420 align:middle line:84%
But what does this
alternative basis?

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.190 align:middle line:84%
You can look at your
books, you know.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:10.690 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:11.991 align:middle line:90%
It is the state of nature.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:12.492 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.360 align:middle line:90%
What is the state of nature?

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.261 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:18.565 align:middle line:84%
I actually underlined
this pretty extensively.

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:22.902 align:middle line:84%
It is a state of perfect freedom
and a state also of equality.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:23.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.138 align:middle line:90%
I remember now.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:28.741 align:middle line:84%
But how can it be a
state of perfect freedom

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:32.545 align:middle line:84%
if anyone can take your
stuff and it's only up to you

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:34.447 align:middle line:90%
to protect it?

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:35.715 align:middle line:90%
You don't remember?

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:36.816 align:middle line:90%
That's the point.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.585 align:middle line:84%
There is no government,
so we're all

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:40.453 align:middle line:90%
perfectly free in that sense.

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:42.989 align:middle line:84%
But life is not
desirable since we're

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:45.258 align:middle line:90%
all individually vulnerable.

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:48.027 align:middle line:84%
That's why we form
governments, for security.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.697 align:middle line:84%
Very good, Sophie, but
you're moving too fast.

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:54.067 align:middle line:84%
Remember Locke says but though
this be a state of liberty,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.436 align:middle line:84%
yet it is not a
state of license.

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:58.037 align:middle line:90%
Do you recall why that is?

00:03:58.037 --> 00:03:59.105 align:middle line:90%
Oh, hold on.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.240 align:middle line:90%
I have it right here.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.610 align:middle line:90%
Because of a law of nature.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.444 align:middle line:90%
Yes, that's it.

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:07.013 align:middle line:84%
Laws of nature are
based on reason.

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:07.647 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.515 align:middle line:84%
We talked about that
before spring break.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:10.717 align:middle line:90%
Now I remember.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:14.353 align:middle line:84%
It's dumb to waste stuff,
including your own life.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:16.255 align:middle line:84%
What about these people
that waste their money

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.791 align:middle line:84%
on luxuries, expensive
cars and things?

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:19.358 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:21.628 align:middle line:84%
I'd be content with
a cheap rundown car.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:23.863 align:middle line:84%
Why should someone buy a
Ferrari when most of us

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:25.331 align:middle line:90%
can't afford an old Kia?

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:29.535 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone recall
Locke's answer to this?

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:32.805 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps, once again,
we are moving too fast.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:36.242 align:middle line:90%
Let's go step by step.

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:40.246 align:middle line:84%
What is the state
of nature like?

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:40.813 align:middle line:90%
Alvis.

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:43.816 align:middle line:84%
In addition to freedom and
equality, there is a law.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:44.317 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:46.052 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.287 align:middle line:90%
There is punishment.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:50.590 align:middle line:84%
In paragraph seven, he
says everyone has a right

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.826 align:middle line:84%
to punish the transgressors
of that law to such a degree

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.728 align:middle line:90%
as may hinder its violation.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:56.996 align:middle line:90%
Very good, Sophie.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.099 align:middle line:84%
I think we will see that
our objection to bias

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:03.136 align:middle line:84%
is itself based on a sense
that there is a law of nature.

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.772 align:middle line:84%
How does Locke characterize
this statement?

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:10.743 align:middle line:84%
He says in paragraph nine that
this will seem a very strange

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:12.879 align:middle line:90%
doctrine to some.

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:13.780 align:middle line:90%
Excellent.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.150 align:middle line:90%
Does it seem strange to you?

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.353 align:middle line:84%
Well, not any more strange
than the state of nature.

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:22.422 align:middle line:84%
Why is the state
of nature strange?

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:25.725 align:middle line:84%
So nature is what you
experience when you go on a hike

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:27.527 align:middle line:90%
or go camping.

00:05:27.527 --> 00:05:30.663 align:middle line:84%
What Locke is describing
never really existed.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.099 align:middle line:84%
What do you think
existed in its place?

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:35.034 align:middle line:84%
So humans evolved
from humanoids,

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.669 align:middle line:90%
and they lived in tribes.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.237 align:middle line:90%
They weren't by themselves.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:39.072 align:middle line:90%
Right, Patch.

00:05:39.072 --> 00:05:42.642 align:middle line:84%
But what is the basis of the
rule of the tribe leader?

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:43.609 align:middle line:90%
Power.

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.312 align:middle line:84%
But isn't this exactly what
Locke said at the beginning?

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:48.648 align:middle line:84%
We can have tribal
rule based on power,

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:51.517 align:middle line:84%
or we can have an alternative,
which Locke provides.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:55.321 align:middle line:84%
Besides, don't you think
human beings' natural state is

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:58.257 align:middle line:90%
freedom and equality?

00:05:58.257 --> 00:05:59.225 align:middle line:90%
That all makes sense.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:03.296 align:middle line:84%
But then why does Locke call
it a very strange doctrine?

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:04.530 align:middle line:90%
To some.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.300 align:middle line:84%
See, I think that will
find that what is required

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:11.137 align:middle line:84%
is not so much a new doctrine,
say a learned latent structure

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:15.007 align:middle line:84%
for algorithmic bias mitigation
or domain adaptation,

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:18.010 align:middle line:84%
but a reflection
on what doctrine

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.847 align:middle line:84%
some find strange
and others do not.

00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:25.284 align:middle line:84%
What's strange to us actually
contradicts our own biases.

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:29.055 align:middle line:84%
Psychologically, we tend
to resist those things

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:32.959 align:middle line:84%
because they point out
our biases to ourselves.

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:35.294 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:40.199 align:middle line:84%
What we find strange is an
indication of our own bias.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.301 align:middle line:90%
That's what Locke is saying.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.970 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, Professor Knowles.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.239 align:middle line:84%
And I realize that he
defends this doctrine

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:48.808 align:middle line:84%
by arguing that governments
can punish foreigners

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:51.511 align:middle line:84%
who break a law,
which is related

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:54.447 align:middle line:84%
to the example in
paragraph 14, when he says

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:59.252 align:middle line:84%
that by the state of nature
exists today, since all princes

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.220 align:middle line:84%
and rulers of
independent governments

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.023 align:middle line:84%
all through the world
are in a state of nature.

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:07.560 align:middle line:84%
Very good, and then he
brings in the next paragraph

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.695 align:middle line:84%
the weight of the writings
of Hooker, or shall

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:11.764 align:middle line:90%
we say the judicious Hooker.

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:15.301 align:middle line:84%
And he ends the chapter
with the idea of consent.

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:19.238 align:middle line:84%
So political society is made
legitimate only by consent.